<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Hasinai Confederacy</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1859 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Caddo confederacy of east Texas whose word for friend, taysha, gave the name Texas to a Spanish province and a modern state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Hasinai were one of three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caddo">Caddo</a> confederacies, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadohadacho">Kadohadacho</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natchitoches-confederacy">Natchitoches</a>, descended from mound-building Caddoan cultures that flourished from around 800 in the forests where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> meet. By about 1500 several Hasinai communities along the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neches-river">Neches</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angelina-river">Angelina</a> rivers had formed a loose alliance.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A religious leader, the xinesi, kept the sacred fire in a temple, while village chiefs, the caddi, governed with the help of officials. Dispersed farmsteads of tall grass houses grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caddo">Caddo</a> made fine engraved pottery and traded salt and bows. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-texas">Spanish Texas</a> began with the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> de los Tejas among the Hasinai in 1690, and after 1714 they traded guns and horses with the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natchitoches-confederacy">Natchitoches</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Epidemics sharply reduced the Hasinai in the 18th century. After Texan independence they were pushed onto the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazos-reserve">Brazos Reserve</a> in 1855 and in 1859 removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>. Their descendants form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caddo">Caddo</a> Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hasinai-confederacy.jpg" alt="Hasinai Confederacy" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Uyvsdi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caddo_cultural_club.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1859 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Caddo traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Caddo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Caddo Confederacy, Tejas, Hasinai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1680&amp;focus=hasinai-confederacy" aria-label="Hasinai Confederacy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2288 767 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hasinai Confederacy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2608 1023h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2608 1023h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United States. <a href="/atlas?year=1680&amp;focus=hasinai-confederacy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

